[2026-03-14T20:45:35.245Z] {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01LhDc4nWws5fs8AbVDLmTxY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013AorvnFNYFsehZhRb9DDm1","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/repo/skills/scripts/deploy.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6639,"cache_read_input_tokens":14255,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6639,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"17b27623-7930-4e57-8201-63e9edf08d5d","uuid":"51f5dde5-01d1-4f1f-91d9-88c3021b29a2"}
[2026-03-14T20:45:35.737Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013AorvnFNYFsehZhRb9DDm1","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# deploy\n     2→\n     3→## Purpose\n     4→\n     5→Creates and/or syncs the production Neon database and creates and/or updates the Netlify site.\n     6→This is the single command for deploying the app to production. All project information\n     7→(database URL, Netlify site ID, etc.) is persisted in `.env` so subsequent runs reuse existing\n     8→resources.\n     9→\n    10→## Usage\n    11→\n    12→- `package.json` entry: `\"deploy\": \"tsx scripts/deploy.ts\"`\n    13→- No command-line arguments.\n    14→- Example: `npm run deploy`\n    15→\n    16→## Behavior\n    17→\n    18→### Database setup (first run)\n    19→\n    20→1. Check `.env` for an existing `NEON_PROJECT_ID`. If not present:\n    21→   a. Create a new Neon project via the Neon API.\n    22→   b. Write `NEON_PROJECT_ID` and `DATABASE_URL` to `.env`.\n    23→\n    24→### Database schema sync (every run)\n    25→\n    26→2. Call `initSchema(productionDatabaseUrl)` to create any missing tables.\n    27→3. Run migrations (`migrate-db` logic) against the production database to handle `ALTER TABLE`\n    28→   changes that `initSchema` cannot detect.\n    29→\n    30→### Seed data (first deployment only)\n    31→\n    32→4. On the **first deployment** (i.e. the database was just created), insert seed data that\n    33→   demonstrates the app's features. The seed data should be realistic and cover the main\n    34→   entities and relationships so a user can immediately explore the app without needing to\n    35→   create everything from scratch. Skip seeding on subsequent deployments.\n    36→\n    37→### Netlify site setup (first run)\n    38→\n    39→5. Check `.env` for an existing `NETLIFY_SITE_ID`. If not present:\n    40→   a. Create a new Netlify site via `netlify sites:create`.\n    41→   b. Write `NETLIFY_SITE_ID` to `.env`.\n    42→\n    43→### Build and deploy (every run)\n    44→\n    45→6. Build the app (`vite build`). Pipe build output to the log file.\n    46→7. Deploy to Netlify (`netlify deploy --prod`). Pipe deploy output to the log file.\n    47→8. Write the deployed URL, `site_id`, `neon_project_id`, and `database_url` to the top of `deployment.txt`\n    48→   (overwriting the previous resource block but preserving any deployment history entries below).\n    49→9. Print a one-line summary to stdout:\n    50→   - Success: `Deployed to <url>`\n    51→   - Failure: `Deploy failed (build|netlify) — see logs/deploy.log`\n    52→\n    53→## Populating `.env` for Redeployments\n    54→\n    55→`.env` is gitignored and will not exist in a fresh environment. If the app has been\n    56→deployed before, you MUST populate `.env` before running the script so it reuses the\n    57→existing Netlify site and Neon database instead of creating new ones.\n    58→\n    59→Read `deployment.txt` (committed to git) to get all previously stored deployment\n    60→values (`site_id`, `neon_project_id`, `database_url`). Write them to `.env`:\n    61→\n    62→```\n    63→NETLIFY_SITE_ID=<site_id from deployment.txt>\n    64→NEON_PROJECT_ID=<neon_project_id from deployment.txt>\n    65→DATABASE_URL=<database_url from deployment.txt>\n    66→```\n    67→\n    68→If `.env` is missing these values the script will create **new** resources, which means\n    69→a new URL and an empty database. Always check `deployment.txt` first.\n    70→\n    71→## Inputs\n    72→\n    73→- **Environment variables**:\n    74→  - `NEON_API_KEY` (required): For Neon project/database management.\n    75→  - `NETLIFY_AUTH_TOKEN` (required): For Netlify CLI authentication.\n    76→  - `NETLIFY_ACCOUNT_SLUG` (required): For Netlify site creation.\n    77→- **Files**:\n    78→  - `.env`: Read for existing project info (`NEON_PROJECT_ID`, `DATABASE_URL`,\n    79→    `NETLIFY_SITE_ID`). Written to on first run.\n    80→  - `deployment.txt`: Resource block at the top contains `site_id`, `neon_project_id`, and\n    81→    `database_url` from the last deployment (committed to git). Use this to populate `.env`\n    82→    when deploying in a fresh environment. Deployment history entries follow below.\n    83→\n    84→## Outputs\n    85→\n    86→- **stdout**: One-line summary only.\n    87→- **`logs/deploy.log`**: Full build and deploy output. Overwritten each run.\n    88→- **`.env`**: Updated with `NEON_PROJECT_ID`, `DATABASE_URL`, `NETLIFY_SITE_ID` if created.\n    89→- **`deployment.txt`**: Resource block at the top is updated with the current deployed URL,\n    90→  `site_id`, `neon_project_id`, and `database_url`. Deployment history entries below the\n    91→  resource block are preserved. The deployment skill appends a new history entry after\n    92→  each successful deploy.\n    93→- **Side effects**:\n    94→  - Creates Neon project (first run only).\n    95→  - Syncs production database schema (every run).\n    96→  - Creates Netlify site (first run only).\n    97→  - Deploys built app to Netlify (every run).\n    98→- **Exit codes**:\n    99→  - 0: Deployment succeeded.\n   100→  - Non-zero: A step failed.\n   101→\n   102→## SPA Routing\n   103→\n   104→Single-page apps require a `_redirects` file so that Netlify serves `index.html` for all\n   105→client-side routes. Without this, direct navigation to `/dashboard` or other routes returns 404.\n   106→\n   107→Create `public/_redirects` with:\n   108→\n   109→```\n   110→/* /index.html 200\n   111→```\n   112→\n   113→The deploy script should ensure this file exists before building. If the app uses client-side\n   114→routing (React Router, etc.), this file is mandatory.\n   115→\n   116→## Post-Deploy Checklist\n   117→\n   118→After the first successful deployment, verify that required environment variables are set on\n   119→the Netlify site. Missing env vars cause production 500 errors that are hard to diagnose.\n   120→\n   121→**IMPORTANT: Check for account-level env var overrides.** Netlify account-level environment\n   122→variables take precedence over site-level variables. If `DATABASE_URL` is set at the account\n   123→level, it will override the site-level value, causing the production app to connect to the\n   124→wrong database. To check and fix:\n   125→\n   126→```bash\n   127→# Check account-level env vars\n   128→curl -s -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NETLIFY_AUTH_TOKEN\" \\\n   129→  \"https://api.netlify.com/api/v1/accounts/$NETLIFY_ACCOUNT_SLUG/env\" | python3 -c \"\n   130→import sys, json\n   131→for v in json.load(sys.stdin):\n   132→    print(v['key'], '=', v['values'][0]['value'][:20] if v['values'] else '(empty)')\n   133→\"\n   134→\n   135→# If DATABASE_URL exists at account level, delete or override it at site level\n   136→# Use context \"production\" (not \"all\") when setting site-level overrides\n   137→curl -s -X PATCH -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NETLIFY_AUTH_TOKEN\" \\\n   138→  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n   139→  \"https://api.netlify.com/api/v1/accounts/$NETLIFY_ACCOUNT_SLUG/env/DATABASE_URL\" \\\n   140→  -d '{\"context\":\"production\",\"value\":\"<correct-database-url>\"}'\n   141→```\n   142→\n   143→Use the Netlify REST API to set environment variables — the CLI `npx netlify env:set --site`\n   144→flag is unreliable and silently fails in many environments. See `skills/scripts/netlify-env.md`\n   145→for the working REST API approach.\n   146→\n   147→1. **Check existing env vars**: Use the Netlify REST API or `LC_ALL=C npx netlify env:list --json --site $NETLIFY_SITE_ID`\n   148→2. **Set `DATABASE_URL`**: See `skills/scripts/netlify-env.md` for the REST API command.\n   149→   The deploy script writes `DATABASE_URL` to `.env` but does NOT automatically set it on Netlify.\n   150→   You must set it manually after the first deploy.\n   151→3. **Run the deployment test** (`npx playwright test --config playwright.deployment.config.ts`)\n   152→   to confirm the production app can load data and perform writes.\n   153→\n   154→## Exporting `.env` for Shell Commands\n   155→\n   156→`source .env` does NOT export variables — they are only available in the current shell, not in\n   157→subprocesses or `curl` commands. When you need `.env` values in shell commands, use:\n   158→\n   159→```bash\n   160→export $(grep -v '^#' .env | xargs)\n   161→```\n   162→\n   163→This exports all non-comment lines as environment variables accessible to subprocesses.\n   164→\n   165→## Locale Workaround\n   166→\n   167→The Netlify CLI requires a valid locale. In containers that lack `en_US.UTF-8`, CLI commands\n   168→(`netlify deploy`, `netlify sites:create`) fail with locale errors. Always prefix Netlify CLI\n   169→commands with `LC_ALL=C` to avoid this:\n   170→\n   171→```bash\n   172→LC_ALL=C npx netlify deploy --prod --dir dist --functions ./netlify/functions\n   173→LC_ALL=C npx netlify sites:create --account-slug $NETLIFY_ACCOUNT_SLUG\n   174→```\n   175→\n   176→The deploy script should set `LC_ALL=C` in the environment before spawning Netlify CLI\n   177→subprocesses.\n   178→\n   179→**ANSI code contamination**: `LC_ALL=C` also prevents ANSI escape codes from appearing in\n   180→CLI output. Without it, extracting URLs or site IDs from `netlify deploy` output may capture\n   181→embedded escape sequences that corrupt `.env` values and break subsequent `curl` calls. See\n   182→`skills/scripts/deploy-troubleshooting.md` for details.\n   183→\n   184→## Netlify CLI Troubleshooting\n   185→\n   186→The Netlify CLI (`npx netlify`) can fail in container environments. Common issues:\n   187→\n   188→- **Locale errors**: Always prefix with `LC_ALL=C` (see Locale Workaround above).\n   189→- **`npx netlify` not found or crashes**: Verify `netlify-cli` is installed:\n   190→  `ls node_modules/.bin/netlify 2>/dev/null || npm install netlify-cli --save-dev`\n   191→- **Direct invocation fails**: If `npx netlify` fails, try the full path:\n   192→  `./node_modules/.bin/netlify deploy --prod ...`\n   193→- **Both invocation methods fail**: The CLI may not be installed globally or locally. Install\n   194→  it explicitly: `npm install netlify-cli --save-dev`, then use `npx netlify`.\n   195→- **Interactive prompts** (CRITICAL): Netlify CLI commands like `netlify sites:create` will\n   196→  prompt interactively for missing arguments (e.g., site name), which hangs in non-interactive\n   197→  shells. The deploy script must NEVER produce interactive prompts. Always pass required\n   198→  arguments explicitly (e.g., `--name <site-name>` or `--site <site-id>`). Test the script\n   199→  end-to-end in a non-interactive shell before considering it complete.\n   200→- **Authentication errors**: Verify `NETLIFY_AUTH_TOKEN` is set:\n   201→  `echo $NETLIFY_AUTH_TOKEN | head -c 5`\n   202→- **\"Site not found\" errors on deploy**: If `netlify deploy` fails with a site-not-found error,\n   203→  run `npx netlify link --id $NETLIFY_SITE_ID` before deploying. This writes the site ID to\n   204→  `.netlify/state.json`, which the CLI reads to identify the target site. The site ID can be\n   205→  found in `.env` (`NETLIFY_SITE_ID`) or `deployment.txt` (`site_id`).\n   206→\n   207→All Netlify CLI commands in the deploy script should use `LC_ALL=C` and pipe output to the\n   208→log file rather than inheriting stdio.\n   209→\n   210→## Database Schema Execution\n   211→\n   212→When running schema scripts directly (outside the deploy script), use `npx tsx -e` with an\n   213→inline import rather than `npx tsx scripts/schema.ts`. The direct file invocation has known\n   214→module resolution issues with `@neondatabase/serverless`:\n   215→\n   216→```bash\n   217→# This often fails with module resolution errors:\n   218→npx tsx scripts/schema.ts\n   219→\n   220→# This works reliably:\n   221→npx tsx -e \"import { initSchema } from './scripts/schema.ts'; await initSchema(process.env.DATABASE_URL!);\"\n   222→```\n   223→\n   224→The deploy script handles this internally, but if you need to run schema operations manually\n   225→(e.g., during initial setup or debugging), use the inline import pattern.\n   226→\n   227→## JSON Parsing in Shell\n   228→\n   229→**`jq` is not available in the container.** When you need to parse JSON from shell commands\n   230→(e.g., Neon API responses, Netlify CLI output), use `node -e` or `python3 -c` instead.\n   231→Example:\n   232→\n   233→```bash\n   234→curl -s ... | node -e \"process.stdin.on('data',d=>console.log(JSON.parse(d).key))\"\n   235→```\n   236→\n   237→## Implementation Tips\n   238→\n   239→- Reuse `initSchema` from `scripts/schema.ts` for schema sync.\n   240→- Reuse migration logic from `scripts/migrate-db.ts`.\n   241→- Use the Neon REST API (`https://console.neon.tech/api/v2/...`) with `NEON_API_KEY` for\n   242→  project creation. Example:\n   243→  ```bash\n   244→  curl -s -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEON_API_KEY\" \\\n   245→    -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n   246→    -d '{\"project\":{\"name\":\"my-app\"}}' \\\n   247→    https://console.neon.tech/api/v2/projects\n   248→  ```\n   249→- When running one-off database queries with `node -e`, use `--input-type=module` for ESM\n   250→  packages like `@neondatabase/serverless`:\n   251→  ```bash\n   252→  node --input-type=module -e \"import { neon } from '@neondatabase/serverless'; const sql = neon('...'); const r = await sql\\`SELECT 1\\`; console.log(r);\"\n   253→  ```\n   254→  Do NOT use `require()` with ESM-only packages — it will fail.\n   255→- Use `netlify sites:create --account-slug $NETLIFY_ACCOUNT_SLUG` for site creation.\n   256→- Use `netlify deploy --prod --dir dist --functions ./netlify/functions` for deployment.\n   257→  If available, use `--json` to get machine-readable output that avoids ANSI escape code\n   258→  parsing issues.\n   259→- After deployment, verify the site URL returns 200 before proceeding to tests:\n   260→  ```bash\n   261→  curl -s -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code}\" <deployed-url>\n   262→  ```\n   263→- Do NOT inherit stdio from subprocesses. Pipe all subprocess output to `logs/deploy.log`.\n   264→- Read/write `.env` using `fs` — parse as key=value lines, append new entries, don't\n   265→  clobber existing values.\n   266→- Production builds must use `sourcemap: true`, `minify: false`, and the React development\n   267→  bundle (see `vite.config.ts` settings) so Replay recordings show readable source.\n   268→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"17b27623-7930-4e57-8201-63e9edf08d5d","uuid":"3ad83865-1927-49d8-ac90-9af31aaf2467","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/repo/skills/scripts/deploy.md","content":"# deploy\n\n## Purpose\n\nCreates and/or syncs the production Neon database and creates and/or updates the Netlify site.\nThis is the single command for deploying the app to production. All project information\n(database URL, Netlify site ID, etc.) is persisted in `.env` so subsequent runs reuse existing\nresources.\n\n## Usage\n\n- `package.json` entry: `\"deploy\": \"tsx scripts/deploy.ts\"`\n- No command-line arguments.\n- Example: `npm run deploy`\n\n## Behavior\n\n### Database setup (first run)\n\n1. Check `.env` for an existing `NEON_PROJECT_ID`. If not present:\n   a. Create a new Neon project via the Neon API.\n   b. Write `NEON_PROJECT_ID` and `DATABASE_URL` to `.env`.\n\n### Database schema sync (every run)\n\n2. Call `initSchema(productionDatabaseUrl)` to create any missing tables.\n3. Run migrations (`migrate-db` logic) against the production database to handle `ALTER TABLE`\n   changes that `initSchema` cannot detect.\n\n### Seed data (first deployment only)\n\n4. On the **first deployment** (i.e. the database was just created), insert seed data that\n   demonstrates the app's features. The seed data should be realistic and cover the main\n   entities and relationships so a user can immediately explore the app without needing to\n   create everything from scratch. Skip seeding on subsequent deployments.\n\n### Netlify site setup (first run)\n\n5. Check `.env` for an existing `NETLIFY_SITE_ID`. If not present:\n   a. Create a new Netlify site via `netlify sites:create`.\n   b. Write `NETLIFY_SITE_ID` to `.env`.\n\n### Build and deploy (every run)\n\n6. Build the app (`vite build`). Pipe build output to the log file.\n7. Deploy to Netlify (`netlify deploy --prod`). Pipe deploy output to the log file.\n8. Write the deployed URL, `site_id`, `neon_project_id`, and `database_url` to the top of `deployment.txt`\n   (overwriting the previous resource block but preserving any deployment history entries below).\n9. Print a one-line summary to stdout:\n   - Success: `Deployed to <url>`\n   - Failure: `Deploy failed (build|netlify) — see logs/deploy.log`\n\n## Populating `.env` for Redeployments\n\n`.env` is gitignored and will not exist in a fresh environment. If the app has been\ndeployed before, you MUST populate `.env` before running the script so it reuses the\nexisting Netlify site and Neon database instead of creating new ones.\n\nRead `deployment.txt` (committed to git) to get all previously stored deployment\nvalues (`site_id`, `neon_project_id`, `database_url`). Write them to `.env`:\n\n```\nNETLIFY_SITE_ID=<site_id from deployment.txt>\nNEON_PROJECT_ID=<neon_project_id from deployment.txt>\nDATABASE_URL=<database_url from deployment.txt>\n```\n\nIf `.env` is missing these values the script will create **new** resources, which means\na new URL and an empty database. Always check `deployment.txt` first.\n\n## Inputs\n\n- **Environment variables**:\n  - `NEON_API_KEY` (required): For Neon project/database management.\n  - `NETLIFY_AUTH_TOKEN` (required): For Netlify CLI authentication.\n  - `NETLIFY_ACCOUNT_SLUG` (required): For Netlify site creation.\n- **Files**:\n  - `.env`: Read for existing project info (`NEON_PROJECT_ID`, `DATABASE_URL`,\n    `NETLIFY_SITE_ID`). Written to on first run.\n  - `deployment.txt`: Resource block at the top contains `site_id`, `neon_project_id`, and\n    `database_url` from the last deployment (committed to git). Use this to populate `.env`\n    when deploying in a fresh environment. Deployment history entries follow below.\n\n## Outputs\n\n- **stdout**: One-line summary only.\n- **`logs/deploy.log`**: Full build and deploy output. Overwritten each run.\n- **`.env`**: Updated with `NEON_PROJECT_ID`, `DATABASE_URL`, `NETLIFY_SITE_ID` if created.\n- **`deployment.txt`**: Resource block at the top is updated with the current deployed URL,\n  `site_id`, `neon_project_id`, and `database_url`. Deployment history entries below the\n  resource block are preserved. The deployment skill appends a new history entry after\n  each successful deploy.\n- **Side effects**:\n  - Creates Neon project (first run only).\n  - Syncs production database schema (every run).\n  - Creates Netlify site (first run only).\n  - Deploys built app to Netlify (every run).\n- **Exit codes**:\n  - 0: Deployment succeeded.\n  - Non-zero: A step failed.\n\n## SPA Routing\n\nSingle-page apps require a `_redirects` file so that Netlify serves `index.html` for all\nclient-side routes. Without this, direct navigation to `/dashboard` or other routes returns 404.\n\nCreate `public/_redirects` with:\n\n```\n/* /index.html 200\n```\n\nThe deploy script should ensure this file exists before building. If the app uses client-side\nrouting (React Router, etc.), this file is mandatory.\n\n## Post-Deploy Checklist\n\nAfter the first successful deployment, verify that required environment variables are set on\nthe Netlify site. Missing env vars cause production 500 errors that are hard to diagnose.\n\n**IMPORTANT: Check for account-level env var overrides.** Netlify account-level environment\nvariables take precedence over site-level variables. If `DATABASE_URL` is set at the account\nlevel, it will override the site-level value, causing the production app to connect to the\nwrong database. To check and fix:\n\n```bash\n# Check account-level env vars\ncurl -s -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NETLIFY_AUTH_TOKEN\" \\\n  \"https://api.netlify.com/api/v1/accounts/$NETLIFY_ACCOUNT_SLUG/env\" | python3 -c \"\nimport sys, json\nfor v in json.load(sys.stdin):\n    print(v['key'], '=', v['values'][0]['value'][:20] if v['values'] else '(empty)')\n\"\n\n# If DATABASE_URL exists at account level, delete or override it at site level\n# Use context \"production\" (not \"all\") when setting site-level overrides\ncurl -s -X PATCH -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NETLIFY_AUTH_TOKEN\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  \"https://api.netlify.com/api/v1/accounts/$NETLIFY_ACCOUNT_SLUG/env/DATABASE_URL\" \\\n  -d '{\"context\":\"production\",\"value\":\"<correct-database-url>\"}'\n```\n\nUse the Netlify REST API to set environment variables — the CLI `npx netlify env:set --site`\nflag is unreliable and silently fails in many environments. See `skills/scripts/netlify-env.md`\nfor the working REST API approach.\n\n1. **Check existing env vars**: Use the Netlify REST API or `LC_ALL=C npx netlify env:list --json --site $NETLIFY_SITE_ID`\n2. **Set `DATABASE_URL`**: See `skills/scripts/netlify-env.md` for the REST API command.\n   The deploy script writes `DATABASE_URL` to `.env` but does NOT automatically set it on Netlify.\n   You must set it manually after the first deploy.\n3. **Run the deployment test** (`npx playwright test --config playwright.deployment.config.ts`)\n   to confirm the production app can load data and perform writes.\n\n## Exporting `.env` for Shell Commands\n\n`source .env` does NOT export variables — they are only available in the current shell, not in\nsubprocesses or `curl` commands. When you need `.env` values in shell commands, use:\n\n```bash\nexport $(grep -v '^#' .env | xargs)\n```\n\nThis exports all non-comment lines as environment variables accessible to subprocesses.\n\n## Locale Workaround\n\nThe Netlify CLI requires a valid locale. In containers that lack `en_US.UTF-8`, CLI commands\n(`netlify deploy`, `netlify sites:create`) fail with locale errors. Always prefix Netlify CLI\ncommands with `LC_ALL=C` to avoid this:\n\n```bash\nLC_ALL=C npx netlify deploy --prod --dir dist --functions ./netlify/functions\nLC_ALL=C npx netlify sites:create --account-slug $NETLIFY_ACCOUNT_SLUG\n```\n\nThe deploy script should set `LC_ALL=C` in the environment before spawning Netlify CLI\nsubprocesses.\n\n**ANSI code contamination**: `LC_ALL=C` also prevents ANSI escape codes from appearing in\nCLI output. Without it, extracting URLs or site IDs from `netlify deploy` output may capture\nembedded escape sequences that corrupt `.env` values and break subsequent `curl` calls. See\n`skills/scripts/deploy-troubleshooting.md` for details.\n\n## Netlify CLI Troubleshooting\n\nThe Netlify CLI (`npx netlify`) can fail in container environments. Common issues:\n\n- **Locale errors**: Always prefix with `LC_ALL=C` (see Locale Workaround above).\n- **`npx netlify` not found or crashes**: Verify `netlify-cli` is installed:\n  `ls node_modules/.bin/netlify 2>/dev/null || npm install netlify-cli --save-dev`\n- **Direct invocation fails**: If `npx netlify` fails, try the full path:\n  `./node_modules/.bin/netlify deploy --prod ...`\n- **Both invocation methods fail**: The CLI may not be installed globally or locally. Install\n  it explicitly: `npm install netlify-cli --save-dev`, then use `npx netlify`.\n- **Interactive prompts** (CRITICAL): Netlify CLI commands like `netlify sites:create` will\n  prompt interactively for missing arguments (e.g., site name), which hangs in non-interactive\n  shells. The deploy script must NEVER produce interactive prompts. Always pass required\n  arguments explicitly (e.g., `--name <site-name>` or `--site <site-id>`). Test the script\n  end-to-end in a non-interactive shell before considering it complete.\n- **Authentication errors**: Verify `NETLIFY_AUTH_TOKEN` is set:\n  `echo $NETLIFY_AUTH_TOKEN | head -c 5`\n- **\"Site not found\" errors on deploy**: If `netlify deploy` fails with a site-not-found error,\n  run `npx netlify link --id $NETLIFY_SITE_ID` before deploying. This writes the site ID to\n  `.netlify/state.json`, which the CLI reads to identify the target site. The site ID can be\n  found in `.env` (`NETLIFY_SITE_ID`) or `deployment.txt` (`site_id`).\n\nAll Netlify CLI commands in the deploy script should use `LC_ALL=C` and pipe output to the\nlog file rather than inheriting stdio.\n\n## Database Schema Execution\n\nWhen running schema scripts directly (outside the deploy script), use `npx tsx -e` with an\ninline import rather than `npx tsx scripts/schema.ts`. The direct file invocation has known\nmodule resolution issues with `@neondatabase/serverless`:\n\n```bash\n# This often fails with module resolution errors:\nnpx tsx scripts/schema.ts\n\n# This works reliably:\nnpx tsx -e \"import { initSchema } from './scripts/schema.ts'; await initSchema(process.env.DATABASE_URL!);\"\n```\n\nThe deploy script handles this internally, but if you need to run schema operations manually\n(e.g., during initial setup or debugging), use the inline import pattern.\n\n## JSON Parsing in Shell\n\n**`jq` is not available in the container.** When you need to parse JSON from shell commands\n(e.g., Neon API responses, Netlify CLI output), use `node -e` or `python3 -c` instead.\nExample:\n\n```bash\ncurl -s ... | node -e \"process.stdin.on('data',d=>console.log(JSON.parse(d).key))\"\n```\n\n## Implementation Tips\n\n- Reuse `initSchema` from `scripts/schema.ts` for schema sync.\n- Reuse migration logic from `scripts/migrate-db.ts`.\n- Use the Neon REST API (`https://console.neon.tech/api/v2/...`) with `NEON_API_KEY` for\n  project creation. Example:\n  ```bash\n  curl -s -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEON_API_KEY\" \\\n    -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n    -d '{\"project\":{\"name\":\"my-app\"}}' \\\n    https://console.neon.tech/api/v2/projects\n  ```\n- When running one-off database queries with `node -e`, use `--input-type=module` for ESM\n  packages like `@neondatabase/serverless`:\n  ```bash\n  node --input-type=module -e \"import { neon } from '@neondatabase/serverless'; const sql = neon('...'); const r = await sql\\`SELECT 1\\`; console.log(r);\"\n  ```\n  Do NOT use `require()` with ESM-only packages — it will fail.\n- Use `netlify sites:create --account-slug $NETLIFY_ACCOUNT_SLUG` for site creation.\n- Use `netlify deploy --prod --dir dist --functions ./netlify/functions` for deployment.\n  If available, use `--json` to get machine-readable output that avoids ANSI escape code\n  parsing issues.\n- After deployment, verify the site URL returns 200 before proceeding to tests:\n  ```bash\n  curl -s -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code}\" <deployed-url>\n  ```\n- Do NOT inherit stdio from subprocesses. Pipe all subprocess output to `logs/deploy.log`.\n- Read/write `.env` using `fs` — parse as key=value lines, append new entries, don't\n  clobber existing values.\n- Production builds must use `sourcemap: true`, `minify: false`, and the React development\n  bundle (see `vite.config.ts` settings) so Replay recordings show readable source.\n","numLines":268,"startLine":1,"totalLines":268}}}
