[2026-03-14T20:49:45.560Z] {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011osiunh8uQJbnHkDZAnRLv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Nf7j2UYYEaQ329NhPvu11D","name":"WebFetch","input":{"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-mcp/SKILL.md","prompt":"Extract the full content about how to use Replay MCP tools to analyze recordings"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1404,"cache_read_input_tokens":41313,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1404,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"17b27623-7930-4e57-8201-63e9edf08d5d","uuid":"2ec1da75-6e1c-4b2c-afd0-4363cf94ccb4"}
[2026-03-14T20:49:48.973Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Nf7j2UYYEaQ329NhPvu11D","type":"tool_result","content":"# How to Use Replay MCP Tools to Analyze Recordings\n\n## Core Methodology\n\nReplay MCP provides a systematic approach to investigating browser recordings. The recommended workflow involves:\n\n1. **Formulate a Question**: Frame your investigation as a specific question needing an answer\n2. **Explore**: Use tools to identify relevant events in the recording\n3. **Explain**: Understand the underlying reasons for those events\n4. **Hypothesize**: Form conclusions supported by gathered data\n5. **Document**: Present findings with supporting evidence\n\nAs stated in the guidance: \"The most important thing to remember when using Replay MCP to investigate a recording is to not jump to conclusions.\"\n\n## Tool Categories\n\n**Exploratory Tools** (for discovery):\n- Error detection (UncaughtException, ReactException)\n- Timeline analysis (console messages, network requests, React renders, local storage)\n- Source code investigation (find, read, and search sources)\n\n**Explanatory Tools** (for understanding):\n- Dependency tracking (stack frames, control flow)\n- State details (point descriptions, element inspection, component trees, expression evaluation)\n\n## Key Techniques\n\nThe guidance emphasizes that \"when understanding JS behavior it is a good idea to find code relevant to the question being answered, see whether that code executed at all, and get details about the times when it executed.\"\n\nThe **Logpoint tool** is highlighted as particularly valuable for tracking expression values across execution points, revealing unexpected patterns for further investigation."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"17b27623-7930-4e57-8201-63e9edf08d5d","uuid":"5c388ee4-8dfb-47f4-8c7f-cc8beb10953b","tool_use_result":{"bytes":4555,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# How to Use Replay MCP Tools to Analyze Recordings\n\n## Core Methodology\n\nReplay MCP provides a systematic approach to investigating browser recordings. The recommended workflow involves:\n\n1. **Formulate a Question**: Frame your investigation as a specific question needing an answer\n2. **Explore**: Use tools to identify relevant events in the recording\n3. **Explain**: Understand the underlying reasons for those events\n4. **Hypothesize**: Form conclusions supported by gathered data\n5. **Document**: Present findings with supporting evidence\n\nAs stated in the guidance: \"The most important thing to remember when using Replay MCP to investigate a recording is to not jump to conclusions.\"\n\n## Tool Categories\n\n**Exploratory Tools** (for discovery):\n- Error detection (UncaughtException, ReactException)\n- Timeline analysis (console messages, network requests, React renders, local storage)\n- Source code investigation (find, read, and search sources)\n\n**Explanatory Tools** (for understanding):\n- Dependency tracking (stack frames, control flow)\n- State details (point descriptions, element inspection, component trees, expression evaluation)\n\n## Key Techniques\n\nThe guidance emphasizes that \"when understanding JS behavior it is a good idea to find code relevant to the question being answered, see whether that code executed at all, and get details about the times when it executed.\"\n\nThe **Logpoint tool** is highlighted as particularly valuable for tracking expression values across execution points, revealing unexpected patterns for further investigation.","durationMs":3411,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-mcp/SKILL.md"}}
[2026-03-14T20:49:50.113Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HUQ95PoHJANkjg98P4P47N","type":"tool_result","content":"# Replay Playwright Setup Guide\n\n## Configuration for playwright.config.ts\n\nTo enable Replay recordings, update your Playwright configuration file with:\n\n```typescript\nimport { replayReporter, devices as replayDevices } from \"@replayio/playwright\";\n\nconst config: PlaywrightTestConfig = {\n  reporter: [\n    replayReporter({\n      apiKey: process.env.REPLAY_API_KEY,\n      upload: true,\n    }),\n    [\"line\"],\n  ],\n  projects: [\n    {\n      name: \"replay-chromium\",\n      use: { ...replayDevices[\"Replay Chromium\"] },\n    },\n  ],\n};\n```\n\n**Key configuration points:**\n- The `replayReporter` manages uploading recordings after test completion\n- Setting `upload: true` ensures recordings upload automatically when tests finish\n- The `replay-chromium` project uses the Replay Browser to capture execution recordings\n- Preserve existing projects and reporters alongside the Replay configuration\n\n## Running Deployment Tests\n\nExecute Playwright tests with Replay Browser using:\n\n```sh\nnpx playwright test --project replay-chromium\n```\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n1. Install the plugin: `npm install --save-dev @replayio/playwright`\n2. Install the browser: `npx replayio install`\n3. Create a Test Suite Team at https://app.replay.io/team/new/tests to obtain an API key\n4. Store your API key as `REPLAY_API_KEY` in your `.env` file or as an environment variable\n\n**Note:** The document provided does not contain specific guidance for a separate `playwright.deployment.config.ts` file."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"17b27623-7930-4e57-8201-63e9edf08d5d","uuid":"5cbbbda6-b047-4cfe-97e4-234756189f29","tool_use_result":{"bytes":5167,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Replay Playwright Setup Guide\n\n## Configuration for playwright.config.ts\n\nTo enable Replay recordings, update your Playwright configuration file with:\n\n```typescript\nimport { replayReporter, devices as replayDevices } from \"@replayio/playwright\";\n\nconst config: PlaywrightTestConfig = {\n  reporter: [\n    replayReporter({\n      apiKey: process.env.REPLAY_API_KEY,\n      upload: true,\n    }),\n    [\"line\"],\n  ],\n  projects: [\n    {\n      name: \"replay-chromium\",\n      use: { ...replayDevices[\"Replay Chromium\"] },\n    },\n  ],\n};\n```\n\n**Key configuration points:**\n- The `replayReporter` manages uploading recordings after test completion\n- Setting `upload: true` ensures recordings upload automatically when tests finish\n- The `replay-chromium` project uses the Replay Browser to capture execution recordings\n- Preserve existing projects and reporters alongside the Replay configuration\n\n## Running Deployment Tests\n\nExecute Playwright tests with Replay Browser using:\n\n```sh\nnpx playwright test --project replay-chromium\n```\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n1. Install the plugin: `npm install --save-dev @replayio/playwright`\n2. Install the browser: `npx replayio install`\n3. Create a Test Suite Team at https://app.replay.io/team/new/tests to obtain an API key\n4. Store your API key as `REPLAY_API_KEY` in your `.env` file or as an environment variable\n\n**Note:** The document provided does not contain specific guidance for a separate `playwright.deployment.config.ts` file.","durationMs":4857,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-playwright/SKILL.md"}}
